    "result_text": "The JUnit test suite is **green** (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\nThe JaCoCo `check` gate on `verify` fails, but that is **pre-existing and unrelated to my change**: the project ships a single black-box acceptance test, so total line coverage is ~15% (branch ~9%) across the whole codebase \u2014 far below the 85%/66% thresholds no matter what. My ~10 added lines don't move a passing gate to failing; the gate was already red by construction. The actual test suite (Surefire) passes.\n\n## What I implemented\n\nThe rule: on owner creation, if **more than 80 owners were already registered on that owner's registration date**, the owner carries `bulkSignupWarning = true`; otherwise `false`. The value is captured at creation and read back via `GET /api/owners/{id}` (matching the acceptance test).\n\nI reused the existing daily-count infrastructure rather than adding a parallel path:\n\n1. **`ClinicServiceImpl.createOwner`** \u2014 reused the already-computed `ownersOnDate` (from `countByRegistrationDate`, the same count the daily-limit rule uses) to set the flag, plus a `BULK_SIGNUP_WARNING_THRESHOLD = 80` constant next to the existing limit constants:\n   ```java\n   owner.setBulkSignupWarning(ownersOnDate > BULK_SIGNUP_WARNING_THRESHOLD);\n   ```\n2. **`Owner` entity** \u2014 added a persisted `Boolean bulkSignupWarning` field (`@Column(name = \"bulk_signup_warning\")`) with getter/setter, mirroring `namesakeCount`. Persisting it (rather than recomputing on read) is correct because the count reflects the day the owner was created, not the day it's read.\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `bulkSignupWarning` as a read-only boolean on the `Owner` response schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it.\n4. **Mapper** \u2014 no change needed; MapStruct auto-maps the matching property name. Likewise the JDBC repo needs no change (it uses `BeanPropertyRowMapper`/`BeanPropertySqlParameterSource`).\n5. **Schema** \u2014 added the `bulk_signup_warning` column to all four DB schemas (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests, h2, mysql, postgres).\n\nEach piece sits in its natural home: the business decision in the service, persistence on the entity/schema, and serialization via the DTO/mapper.",
